.\" jailops-setup.8, installed as /usr/local/share/man/man8/jailops-setup.8
.Dd November 5, 2026
.Dt JAILOPS-SETUP 8
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm jailops-setup
.Nd set a host up for JailOps after the package is installed
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Fl pool Ar pool
.Op Fl n | -dry-run
.Op Fl y | -yes
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
is the one command to run, as root, after
.Ql pkg install jailops .
It does what the manual installation guide describes, in this order, and only
what is not done yet, so it is safe to run again:
.Bl -enum
.It
Loads the kernel modules
.Sy if_bridge , if_epair , pf
and
.Sy zfs
and sets them to load at boot in
.Pa /boot/loader.conf .
Loading
.Sy pf
does not start it.
.It
Runs
.Ql jailops-priv init -pool Ar pool :
the user and group, the datasets and the configuration files.
.It
Turns IP forwarding on now
.Pq Xr sysctl 8
and at boot
.Pq Va gateway_enable , ipv6_gateway_enable .
.It
Puts the three anchor lines of JailOps into
.Pa /etc/pf.conf
(or the file
.Va pf_rules
of
.Xr rc.conf 5
names), loads it and starts
.Xr pf 4 ;
see
.Sx PF .
.It
Enables
.Sy jailops_priv
and
.Sy jailopsd
with the web console
.Pq Va jailopsd_web
in
.Xr rc.conf 5 ,
unless
.Va jailopsd_web
is set already, and starts them, the helper first.
.It
Runs
.Ql jailops doctor .
.It
Offers to create the first administrator
.Pq Ql jailops user create admin --admin --password-prompt
when no user exists and standard input is a terminal.
.It
Prints the address of the web console.
.El
.Pp
Every action is printed on one line starting with
.Ql >
before it runs.
A step that fails is reported with a line starting with
.Ql x ;
the steps after it still run, except after a failed
.Cm init .
.Pp
A host in appliance mode
.Pq Va jailops_appliance
is set up from the web console instead, and
.Nm
refuses to run there.
.Pp
The options are:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl pool Ar pool
The ZFS pool for JailOps.
Needed only when more than one pool is imported; with one pool that one is
taken.
.It Fl n , -dry-run
Print the actions and change nothing.
The new
.Pa pf.conf
is shown and checked with
.Ql pfctl -nf ,
which changes nothing either.
.It Fl y , -yes
Do not ask: apply the pf changes, including starting pf.
Read what a dry run prints first.
.El
.Sh PF
Lines that are in
.Pa pf.conf
are never changed, moved or removed.
What is missing is inserted where pf wants it: options, normalisation,
translation, filtering.
.Bl -bullet
.It
.Ql set state-policy if-bound ,
when no
.Ql set state-policy
line exists, before the first rule.
Jails behind NAT need it.
.It
.Ql scrub in all fragment reassemble ,
when there is no scrub rule, before the first translation or filter rule.
.It
.Ql nat-anchor \&"jailops/*\&"
and
.Ql rdr-anchor \&"jailops/*\&" ,
before the first translation rule, and before the first block or pass rule.
.It
.Ql anchor \&"jailops/*\&" ,
before the first filter rule, so that a broad pass rule of the host does not
decide for the jails.
.El
.Pp
Each inserted line follows a comment that starts with
.Ql # jailops-setup: .
When the file does not exist, a minimal one is written: the options, the three
anchor lines and
.Ql pass all ,
which blocks nothing.
A file that includes other files is not edited when an anchor line is missing:
add the lines by hand.
.Pp
The new text is checked with
.Ql pfctl -nf
before anything is written; when pf refuses it, the file stays as it is and
nothing is loaded.
The old file is kept next to it as
.Pa pf.conf.jailops-setup- Ns Ar YYYYMMDD-HHMMSS ,
with the same mode and owner.
The rules are then loaded with
.Ql pfctl -f ,
which keeps the states of open connections, so a running ssh session stays.
.Nm
never runs
.Ql service pf restart
or flushes states.
.Pp
Starting pf
.Pq Ql pfctl -e
and enabling it at boot
.Pq Va pf_enable
are said before they happen.
When the rules contain a block rule,
.Nm
warns that an ssh session is cut unless a pass rule admits it.
Every pf change is asked for, and the answer is no unless it is
.Ql y ;
.Fl y
answers yes.
.Sh ENVIRONMENT
For tests only:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Ev JAILOPS_SETUP_ROOT
A prefix of every file path read or written.
.It Ev JAILOPS_SETUP_PRIV
The
.Nm jailops-priv
binary.
.It Ev JAILOPS_SETUP_WAIT
Seconds to wait for the socket of the daemon, default 20.
.El
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width Ds -compact
.It Pa /etc/pf.conf
The rules of pf, with the anchor lines of JailOps.
.It Pa /etc/rc.conf
The variables of the services, forwarding and pf.
.It Pa /boot/loader.conf
The kernel modules loaded at boot.
.It Pa /usr/local/libexec/jailops-priv
The helper that runs
.Cm init .
.El
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Bl -tag -width Ds -compact
.It 0
Everything is set up, or a dry run.
.It 1
Not root, no pool, appliance mode, or a step is not done (a refused pf change
included); the lines starting with
.Ql x
say which.
.It 2
Usage, an unknown pool, or more than one pool and no
.Fl pool .
.El
.Sh EXAMPLES
See what would be done, then do it:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
jailops-setup -n
jailops-setup
.Ed
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr jailops 1 ,
.Xr pf.conf 5 ,
.Xr rc.conf 5 ,
.Xr jailops-priv 8 ,
.Xr jailopsd 8 ,
.Xr pfctl 8
